#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166468 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5802583-df2c-5f08-bf9f-8b36247e34d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :question/id #uuid "2e24d040-266c-5ba5-bb2b-9918c22eb48e" :question/math-academy-id "e-21596" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 4x(t); y^{′}(t) =-6y(t)$\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/3e95cfcc1b3c61d0939dcf24b15dc270.png)" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-6$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{4t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach infinity as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-6t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nAmong the given options, the only phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/606b8a3c79391a14d997ef7231295e1e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166469 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d81d981f-ab55-5637-bff5-ae47633b0b79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the System Given a Phase Portrait"}] :question/id #uuid "d4296f42-4f86-5ef8-813e-261afa85b829" :question/math-academy-id "e-21597" :question/problem "Consider the phase portrait for a decoupled system above.\nWhich of the following systems could correspond to the given portrait?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/788240fc680483e8d3c84baf05680420.png)\n\n1. ${x^{′}(t) =-5x(t); y^{′}(t) = 6y(t)$\n2. ${x^{′}(t) = 5x(t); y^{′}(t) = 6y(t)$\n3. ${x^{′}(t) = 5x(t); y^{′}(t) =-6y(t)$\n4. ${x^{′}(t) =-5x(t); y^{′}(t) =-6y(t)$" :question/worked-solution "Writing a decoupled system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}λ_{1} & 0 \\\\ 0 & λ_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are parallel to the axes. These correspond to so-called straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The tangents along the $x$-axis are pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$. So, we must have $\\lambda_1 < 0$.\n- The tangents along the $y$-axis are also pointing toward the origin, meaning that the solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nmust also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$. So, we must have $\\lambda_2 < 0$.\n\nAmong the options, the only system that satisfies $\\lambda_1 < 0$ and $\\lambda_2 < 0$ is the following:\n\n$$\n{x^{′}(t) =-5x(t); y^{′}(t) =-6y(t)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166472 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "24bd917d-c4b3-5bf5-9426-a87a579b03c1" :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Decoupled Systems of Linear Differential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "4926ad20-fc6b-5a7d-80ee-aa39421c174d" :question/math-academy-id "e-21607" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) = 2x(t); y^{′}(t) = 4y(t)$\nInterpret the stability of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the equilibrium point (the origin) for the given decoupled system of linear differential equations." :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nRecall that a homogeneous system of linear differential equations has a single equilibrium (fixed point) at the origin.\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=2$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis and move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty$. So, the $x$-axis is the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{unstable}}$ line.\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=4$ with the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ The corresponding straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis and also move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty$. So, the $y$-axis is the $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{unstable}}$ line.\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium point (origin) is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 1pt gainsboro]{\\text{an unstable node (source)}}$.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/fbc80d18c07900cef20d4276d4e2c539.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166474 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c5ee85e-c6ce-5c8e-bb14-d0a32d6810e4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait Given a System: Advanced Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "74560a13-4a23-553d-8c8d-6c040f03a957" :question/math-academy-id "e-21633" :question/problem "${x^{′}(t) =-x(t); y^{′}(t) =-4y(t)$\nWhich of the following could represent the phase portrait of the decoupled system above?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/5b9c9d82e7a3509295ac890d9c2e3e29.png)" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhere $\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvectors are the following:\n\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_1=-1$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Eigenvalue $\\lambda_2=-4$ with the corresponding eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNow, notice that:\n\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{-t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n- The straight-line solutions of the form\n$\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nlie on the $y$-axis. Since $e^{-4t} \\to 0$ as $t \\to \\infty$, these solutions also approach the origin as $t \\to \\infty$.\n\nAmong the given options, there are two phase portraits satisfying these conditions:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/808f8621a6a3c0729a4f09a90e506461.png)\n\nLet's consider the solutions\n\n$$\nx(t) = c_1e^{-t}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny(t)=c_2e^{-4t}\n$$\n\nprovided that\n\n$$\nc_1,c_2 \\neq 0\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx} = (y^{′}(t))/(x^{′}(t)) = ((c_{2}e^{-4t})^{′})/((c_{1}e^{-t})^{′}) = \\frac{-4c_{2}e^{-4t}}{-c_{1}e^{-t}} = \\frac{4c_{2}}{c_{1}}e^{-3t}\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} \\to 0\n$$\n\nas $t \\to \\infty$, meaning that the tangents to the solution become more and more horizontal as the trajectories approach the origin. This corresponds to the following phase portrait:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3242/Source/Images/8c1e97a2503b3ad40f78284d599a6a91.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d5802583-df2c-5f08-bf9f-8b36247e34d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System"}] :questi